            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Spiritual darkness, sin, ignorance of God, divine judgment, and a period of earthly trial or death, often contrasted with the 'day' of salvation and light.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Romans 13:12\",\"1 Thessalonians 5:5-7\",\"John 9:4\",\"John 13:30\",\"Revelation 21:25\",\"Revelation 22:5\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"A time of divine revelation, intimate encounter, or spiritual testing, such as night visions or the mystical 'dark night of the soul'.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 15:12-17\",\"Acts 16:9\",\"Song of Solomon 3:1\",\"Psalm 16:7\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Concealment and secrecy, often for clandestine or hidden actions (such as Nicodemus approaching Jesus).\",\"basis\":[\"John 3:2\",\"John 19:39\",\"Matthew 25:6\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The natural, God-ordained rhythm of rest, peace, and reliance on divine providence.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 1:5\",\"Psalm 104:20\",\"Psalm 19:2\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"While 'night' frequently carries a negative symbolic connotation representing evil, ignorance, and separation from God in the New Testament (especially in Johannine and Pauline literature), it also serves neutrally as a time for divine encounters and resting in God's providence in other biblical contexts.\"}",
